<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Directory.Build.targets — stop node-pty demanding libraries we do not have (spec Section 190)

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, measured rather than guessed:

    node_modules/node-pty/binding.gyp, line 9:   'SpectreMitigation': 'Spectre'

  node-pty ASKS for Spectre-mitigated libraries. node-gyp 9.x ignored that attribute; node-gyp
  12.4.0 — which @electron/rebuild 4.2.1 bundles — writes it into the generated .vcxproj, and
  MSBuild then fails with MSB8040 because the Spectre variant of the v142 libraries is not
  installed. That is the whole of "this has been happening ever since we upgraded": the upgrade in
  Section 173 did not break the compiler, it started honouring a request node-pty always made.

  WHY OVERRIDE RATHER THAN INSTALL THE COMPONENT. Four rounds went into installing it and it is
  still not on disk; the component id is catalogue-specific, vs_installer exits 0 without doing the
  work, and the machine already has a perfectly good v142 toolset (14.29.30133). Disabling the
  requirement is Microsoft's own documented alternative for MSB8040 — "if you don't want to use
  Spectre mitigation, disable the option" — and it needs nothing downloaded.

  WHAT IT COSTS, honestly. Spectre mitigation is a compiler hardening against a speculative-
  execution side channel: /Qspectre inserts barriers on code paths where an attacker-controlled
  index could be used to train a branch predictor. Without it, node-pty's addon is compiled the way
  the overwhelming majority of npm native modules are. The trade is NOT "hardened versus
  unhardened" — it is "an unhardened terminal versus NO TERMINAL", because today the module does
  not build at all and `terminal:create` refuses every call. Worth stating plainly rather than
  leaving as an implication.

  WHY .targets AND NOT .props. MSBuild imports Directory.Build.props BEFORE the project body, so
  the .vcxproj's own <SpectreMitigation>Spectre</SpectreMitigation> would simply overwrite it.
  Directory.Build.targets is imported AFTER, which is what makes this override win.

  SCOPE. MSBuild walks up from the project directory to the first Directory.Build.targets it finds.
  node-gyp generates its projects under node_modules/<pkg>/build/, so this file at the repository
  root covers every native module built inside this checkout and nothing outside it.
-->
<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <SpectreMitigation>false</SpectreMitigation>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
